{
  "chalk": 1,
  "name": "01-vectors.chalk",
  "cells": [
    {
      "src": "Vectors and the dot product",
      "type": "section",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!goal]\n> Add and scale vectors, find lengths, and use the dot product to tell when two vectors are perpendicular.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "A vector is a list of numbers in brackets, `[1, 2, 3]`. Vectors add and scale entry by entry.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "let u = [1, 2, 3]",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "let v = [4, -1, 2]",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "u + v",
      "showWork": true,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "2u",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "In the plane a vector is an arrow from $0$, and adding is putting arrows tip to tail:",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "clock t from 0 to 1\nview -0.8, 5.2, -0.6, 3.6\nU = arrow(0, [3, 1]) thick color 3\nLU = label([3, 1], \"u\") color 3\nV = arrow(0, [1, 2]) thick color 4\nLV = label([1, 2], \"v\") color 4\nVS = arrow(t*[3, 1], t*[3, 1] + [1, 2]) color 4\nW = arrow(0, [4, 3]) thick color 1\nLW = label([4, 3], \"u + v\") color 1\n> show U, LU, V, LV | Two vectors in the plane, $u = (3, 1)$ and $v = (1, 2)$, each an arrow from $0$.\n> show VS; play t to 1 in 2s | Slide $v$ along $u$, without turning it, until it starts where $u$ ends.\n> show W, LW | The sum runs from the start of $u$ to the tip of the moved $v$: $u + v = (4, 3)$, the entries added.",
      "type": "scene",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "[3, 1] + [1, 2]",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "The dot product",
      "type": "section",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!definition] Dot product and length\n> $u \\cdot v = \\sum_i u_i v_i$, and the length of $v$ is $\\lVert v \\rVert = \\sqrt{v \\cdot v}$.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "dot(u, v)",
      "showWork": true,
      "label": null,
      "stepwise": 0
    },
    {
      "src": "norm([3, 4])",
      "showWork": true,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "norm(u)",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!theorem] Angle\n> $u \\cdot v = \\lVert u \\rVert\\, \\lVert v \\rVert \\cos\\theta$, where $\\theta$ is the angle between them. So $u \\cdot v = 0$ exactly when $u$ and $v$ are perpendicular.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "dot([1, 2], [2, -1])",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Why the angle? Drop $v$ straight onto the line of $u$. Its shadow there is $\\lVert v \\rVert \\cos\\theta$ long, and $u \\cdot v$ is $\\lVert u \\rVert$ times that shadow:",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "clock t from 0 to 2pi\nview -3.2, 3.6, -2.4, 2.6\nU = arrow(0, [2.5, 0]) thick color 3\nLU = label([2.5, 0], \"u\") color 3\nV = arrow(0, [2*cos(t), 2*sin(t)]) thick color 4\nLV = label([2*cos(t), 2*sin(t)], \"v\") color 4\nDROP = segment([2*cos(t), 2*sin(t)], [2*cos(t), 0]) dashed color 6\nSH = segment(0, [2*cos(t), 0]) thick color 1\nD = value(dot([2.5, 0], [2*cos(t), 2*sin(t)]), \"u \\cdot v = \")\n> show U, LU, V, LV, D | $u$ is $2.5$ long and $v$ is $2$. Pointing the same way, $u \\cdot v = 2.5 \\cdot 2 = 5$.\n> show DROP, SH; play t to 1 in 2s | Turn $v$. Its shadow on $u$'s line (orange) shortens, and the dot product with it: $2.5$ times the shadow.\n> play t to pi/2 in 2s | At a right angle the shadow is a point: $u \\cdot v = 0$. Perpendicular vectors are exactly those with dot product zero.\n> play t to pi in 2s | Past a right angle the shadow points backwards, and the dot product is negative.\n> play t to 2pi in 4s | Once round: $u \\cdot v = \\lVert u \\rVert \\lVert v \\rVert \\cos\\theta = 5\\cos\\theta$.",
      "type": "scene",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "dot([1, 2, 3], [4, 5, 6])",
      "type": "exercise",
      "prompt": "Compute $(1, 2, 3) \\cdot (4, 5, 6)$.",
      "hints": [
        "Multiply entry by entry and add: $1 \\cdot 4 + 2 \\cdot 5 + 3 \\cdot 6$."
      ],
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "norm([6, 8])",
      "type": "exercise",
      "prompt": "How long is the vector $(6, 8)$?",
      "hints": [
        "$\\sqrt{6^2 + 8^2}$."
      ],
      "hideQuestion": true,
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "dot([2, 3], [3, -2])",
      "type": "exercise",
      "prompt": "Compute $(2, 3) \\cdot (3, -2)$. What does the answer say about the two vectors?",
      "hints": [
        "Zero means perpendicular."
      ],
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!summary]\n> Vectors add and scale entrywise; the dot product turns two vectors into a number that is zero exactly when they are perpendicular.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    }
  ],
  "scenes": []
}
